---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 6: Functions with Arduino">
    <main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 py-12">
        <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
            <div class="text-center mb-8">
                <div class="flex items-center justify-center mb-4">
                    <div class="bg-blue-500 text-white rounded-full w-16 h-16 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-2xl mr-4">6</div>
                    <div class="text-left">
                        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800">Lesson 6: Functions with Arduino</h1>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Organize repeated jobs into clear, reusable blocks of code</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-blue-600 font-medium">32-Lesson Course • Semester 1</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-lg p-4 mb-8">
                <p class="text-blue-900 font-medium">You already know how Arduino makes decisions with conditionals and repeats actions with loops. Now you will learn how to organize code into functions so a program becomes easier to read, reuse, and improve.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Learning Objectives</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">By the end of this Lesson, you will:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Understand what a function is and why programmers use functions</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Write simple functions that perform one clear job</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Call functions from <code>setup()</code> and <code>loop()</code></li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Pass values into a function using parameters</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-green-600 mb-2">Skills Developed:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Breaking a larger task into smaller steps</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Making code easier to read and edit</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Reusing code instead of copying the same lines again and again</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Preparing for more advanced Arduino programs later</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 1: What Is a Function?</h2>

                <div class="mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">A function is a named block of code</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">A function is a group of instructions that performs one job. Arduino already gives you two built-in functions: <code>setup()</code> and <code>loop()</code>. In this lesson, you will start making your own functions too.</p>

                    <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
                        <h4 class="font-semibold text-blue-800 mb-2">Why functions help</h4>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-blue-700">
                            <li><strong>They reduce repetition:</strong> write the code once and call it many times</li>
                            <li><strong>They improve readability:</strong> a name like <code>printResult()</code> explains the job</li>
                            <li><strong>They make editing easier:</strong> change the function once instead of changing repeated code everywhere</li>
                            <li><strong>They help you think clearly:</strong> each function should do one simple task</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">A function definition has a pattern</h3>
                    <pre class="text-green-400 whitespace-pre-wrap bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">{`returnType functionName(parameters) {
  // code the function will run
}`}</pre>

                    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-4">
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                            <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Important parts</h4>
                            <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 text-sm">
                                <li><strong>returnType:</strong> often <code>void</code> for now</li>
                                <li><strong>functionName:</strong> the name you choose</li>
                                <li><strong>parameters:</strong> extra information sent into the function</li>
                                <li><strong>braces:</strong> the lines that belong to the function</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                            <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">What <code>void</code> means</h4>
                            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">For now, most of your functions will use <code>void</code>. That means the function does a job, but it does not send a value back.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 2: Your First Custom Function</h2>

                <div class="mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">Start with a simple printing function</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">You have already used the Serial Monitor many times. Instead of writing the same printing lines over and over, you can place them inside a function.</p>

                    <div class="relative">
                        <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                            📋 Copy
                        </button>
                        <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  printWelcome();
}

void loop() {
}

void printWelcome() {
  Serial.println("Welcome to Lesson 6!");
  Serial.println("We are learning functions.");
}`}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">What happens here?</h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-green-700">
                        <li><strong><code>setup()</code> runs once:</strong> it starts Serial communication and then calls <code>printWelcome()</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>printWelcome()</code> runs its own code:</strong> it prints two lines</li>
                        <li><strong>The function name acts like a command:</strong> when Arduino sees <code>printWelcome();</code>, it jumps to that function and runs it</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 3: Functions Make Repeated Work Easier</h2>

                <div class="mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">Without a function</h3>
                    <pre class="text-green-400 whitespace-pre-wrap bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  Serial.println("=== Result ===");
  Serial.print("Answer: ");
  Serial.println(12);

  Serial.println("=== Result ===");
  Serial.print("Answer: ");
  Serial.println(25);
}`}</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">With a function</h3>
                    <div class="relative">
                        <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                            📋 Copy
                        </button>
                        <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  printResult(12);
  printResult(25);
}

void loop() {
}

void printResult(int answer) {
  Serial.println("=== Result ===");
  Serial.print("Answer: ");
  Serial.println(answer);
}`}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2">New idea: parameters</h4>
                    <p class="text-yellow-700">The <code>answer</code> variable is a parameter. It receives the value sent into the function. When you call <code>printResult(12);</code>, the function uses <code>12</code>. When you call <code>printResult(25);</code>, the same function uses <code>25</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 4: Functions and Calculations</h2>

                <div class="mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">Use a function to display a conversion</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">This connects back to variables and formulas. You still use variables and calculations, but now you organize the work using a function.</p>

                    <div class="relative">
                        <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                            📋 Copy
                        </button>
                        <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  showTemperatureConversion(20.0);
  showTemperatureConversion(25.0);
  showTemperatureConversion(30.0);
}

void loop() {
}

void showTemperatureConversion(float celsius) {
  float fahrenheit = (celsius * 9.0 / 5.0) + 32.0;

  Serial.print(celsius);
  Serial.print(" C = ");
  Serial.print(fahrenheit);
  Serial.println(" F");
}`}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Why this is better</h4>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 text-sm">
                            <li>The formula is written once</li>
                            <li>The print format is written once</li>
                            <li>You can test many values by changing only the function call</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">What to notice</h4>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 text-sm">
                            <li><code>celsius</code> is the parameter</li>
                            <li><code>fahrenheit</code> is created inside the function</li>
                            <li>The function performs both a calculation and clear Serial output</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 5: Functions with Hardware</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Functions are especially helpful with Arduino hardware because they let you give clear names to repeated actions. Instead of repeating `digitalWrite()` lines, you can create functions like <code>turnLedOn()</code> and <code>turnLedOff()</code>.</p>

                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int ledPin = 8;
const int buttonPin = 2;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  checkButton();
}

void checkButton() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);

  if (buttonState == LOW) {
    turnLedOn();
  } else {
    turnLedOff();
  }
}

void turnLedOn() {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
}

void turnLedOff() {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">Why this code is clearer</h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-green-700">
                        <li><strong><code>loop()</code></strong> stays short and easy to read</li>
                        <li><strong><code>checkButton()</code></strong> describes the decision job</li>
                        <li><strong><code>turnLedOn()</code> and <code>turnLedOff()</code></strong> describe the output jobs</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 6: Common Beginner Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 1</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Forgetting the parentheses when calling a function.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 2</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Using a function name that does not describe the job clearly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 3</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Copying repeated code instead of placing it in a function.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 4</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Trying to make one function do too many different jobs.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Hands-On Activity: Function-Based LED Program</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Build or simulate a button-controlled LED program that uses functions. Your program should:</p>
                <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4 text-yellow-900">
                    <ul class="space-y-2">
                        <li>Use a function to check the button</li>
                        <li>Use one function to turn the LED on</li>
                        <li>Use one function to turn the LED off</li>
                        <li>Keep <code>loop()</code> short and easy to read</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Extension: add another function that blinks the LED three times when the button is pressed.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Assessment and Homework</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Lesson 6 quiz topics</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>What a function is</li>
                            <li>How to define and call a function</li>
                            <li>What <code>void</code> means</li>
                            <li>What parameters do</li>
                            <li>How functions help with hardware code</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Homework ideas</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Write one function that prints a welcome message</li>
                            <li>Write one function that prints a score using a parameter</li>
                            <li>Rewrite a repeated LED action using a function</li>
                            <li>Be ready to use functions in bigger projects later</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-green-900">
                    Good programmers name functions clearly and let each function do one simple job.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex justify-between items-center mt-8">
                <div>
                    <a href="/programming/lessons32/Lesson5" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        ← Previous: Lesson 5
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div class="text-center">
                    <a href="/programming/quizzes32/Lesson6" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 10px 16px; border-radius: 6px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 500; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        Take Quiz 📝
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <a href="/programming/lessons32/Lesson7" style="background-color: #2563eb; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #1d4ed8; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#1d4ed8'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#2563eb'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        Next: Lesson 7 →
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>

<script>
declare global {
    interface Window {
        copyCode: (button: HTMLButtonElement) => void;
    }
}

const lesson6Window = window as Window & typeof globalThis;

lesson6Window.copyCode = function(button: HTMLButtonElement) {
    try {
        const parentElement = button.parentElement;
        if (!parentElement) {
            showCopyFeedback(button, false);
            return;
        }

        const codeElement = parentElement.querySelector('code');
        if (!codeElement) {
            showCopyFeedback(button, false);
            return;
        }

        const codeText = codeElement.textContent || codeElement.innerText;

        if (navigator.clipboard && window.isSecureContext) {
            navigator.clipboard.writeText(codeText).then(() => {
                showCopyFeedback(button, true);
            }).catch(() => {
                fallbackCopy(codeText, button);
            });
        } else {
            fallbackCopy(codeText, button);
        }
    } catch (err) {
        showCopyFeedback(button, false);
    }
};

function fallbackCopy(text: string, button: HTMLButtonElement) {
    try {
        const textarea = document.createElement('textarea');
        textarea.value = text;
        textarea.style.position = 'absolute';
        textarea.style.left = '-9999px';
        textarea.style.top = '0';
        textarea.setAttribute('readonly', '');
        document.body.appendChild(textarea);

        textarea.select();
        textarea.setSelectionRange(0, 99999);

        const successful = document.execCommand('copy');
        document.body.removeChild(textarea);

        showCopyFeedback(button, successful);
    } catch (err) {
        showCopyFeedback(button, false);
    }
}

function showCopyFeedback(button: HTMLButtonElement, success: boolean) {
    const originalText = button.textContent;
    const originalBg = button.style.backgroundColor;

    if (success) {
        button.textContent = '✅ Copied!';
        button.style.backgroundColor = '#10b981';
    } else {
        button.textContent = '❌ Failed';
        button.style.backgroundColor = '#dc2626';
    }

    setTimeout((): void => {
        button.textContent = originalText;
        button.style.backgroundColor = originalBg || '#2563eb';
    }, 2000);
}
</script>
